A||||9||Turkey on Rye||Alternator Belt. Any tricks to doing..........||||1||
Z||000000||Turkey on Rye||10-29-2001||05:39 PM||||Hey everyone, i am going to have to loosen and re-adjust my alternator belt tonight because its rubbing against the side of the pulley thing and i was wonedring if there was any easy way of going about doing this. Is it easiest to remove the wheel well lining and go up from the bottom, or go threw the top? Someone please help me out. Thank you for your time.<BR>                         -Chris<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Turkey on Rye||10-29-2001||06:27 PM||||Someone help, please!!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||10-29-2001||06:30 PM||||4or6?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Turkey on Rye||10-29-2001||07:53 PM||||its on a v6, i just need to know where the loosening screw is located and if there is any tricks on how to get to the belt easier. Thanx||||reg||1
Z||000004||Turkey on Rye||10-29-2001||08:29 PM||||ttt||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||10-29-2001||08:35 PM||||Not really an easy way, I do mine from the bottom. (on ramps) Loosen the top pivot bolt, climb under and loosen the adjusting bolt. Use a long screwdriver or prybar or a alternator griping tool to achieve the correct tension. tighten bolts in reverse order.||||reg||1
Z||000006||David Bartlett||10-29-2001||10:18 PM||||Drive 2 wheels up onto a curb, crawl under and losen the bolt, change belt, get someone to tension from the top while you tighten the bolt down... I can do it in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cheever3000||10-29-2001||10:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>...and if there is any tricks on how to get to the belt easier...<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>No way.  From the top, it's way down there, and from the bottom, it's way up there.  Funny how that is, on such a small car!<P>Both top & bottom bolts are 13mm.  On the top, I have to use an extension on my 1/2" drive socket, because there's a heat shield in the way.  It's takes quite a few turns to loosen it much.  On the bottom, once you take out or loosen the bolt, you probably have to tap on the alt with your ratchet or something to get it pivoted.  I did mine the first time recently (and will have to repeat it in a day or two), and removed the wheel and the wheelwell -- but I don't know if you <I>have</I> to.  Stimpy says he doesn't.  <P>I don't enjoy changing them, but it's cheaper than going to the shop.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||10-31-2001||08:55 AM||||On my 85SE v-6 4spd and my 85GT auto I get to the adjusting bolt from underneath.<P>I jack the rear up as high as I can and put jack stands near the front cradle bolts.<P>Laying on a creeper with my feet sticking out the back I can reach up from underneath and get both hands on the bracket and bolt.  I stick my left hand through an access hole where the dust curtain lays on the cradle.  I use a pry bar jammed between the alternator and its bracket to tension the belt.||||reg||1
Z||000009||CJ||10-31-2001||11:55 AM||||I have a 1988 GT with a V-6...I remove the right rear wheel, move the mud flap up, loosen the pivot bolt from above, the adjustment bolt from below.  I have a belt tightening tool I bought years ago that I place between the alternator and crank pulleys.  You expand the length of the tool (like a turnbuckle) to set the belt tension, then tighten the bolts.||||reg||1
